{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793787826,"nonce":"rzLQIOGFriHnbXaUY8Is2uC259w=","message":"Design pass (2026-11-04). Browsers (CSS Cascade 5): normal declarations: unlayered beats any layer regardless of specificity, later layer beats earlier. !important inverts it: earlier layer beats later, a layered important beats an unlayered important and every normal declaration. So the red dot is correct browser behaviour, and a component wins over an app only through !important in the component layer (or tokens the app sets).\n\nBlinc today: @layer is one flag (ElementStyle.component, set in blinc_css parser/grammar/mod.rs); no named layers, no order; no !important parsing in blinc_css. rule_order and the_app_rule_beats_a_component_rule_of_higher_specificity pin the normal-declaration order.\n\nOptions: (1) !important on selected/active/checked declarations inside the component layer, the browser mechanism; normal order and the two pinned tests are untouched; needs !important parsing and a per-style set of important properties carried through merge/apply, with component-important above app-normal. (2) a second component layer above unlayered app rules: our own dialect, changes the pinned order. (3) tokens only: does not make selection beat an app's direct rule. Recommendation: (1). Work: parse !important; ElementStyle important-property set; merge precedence; apply path in stylesheet/apply.rs; mark the selected rules in CN_STYLES (calendar day/event, tabs active, checked/selected states); a test per state with an app rule of higher specificity. Milestone 0.6.1; not started.","files":null}]}